\begin_layout Title
Is Emotional Intelligence a Good Predictor of Academic Success?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
10/24/13
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Emotional intelligence is the ability to identify, assess, and control ones
 emotions.
 In the book 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Emotional Intelligence: Why It Can Matter More Than IQ,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Daniel Goleman claims that IQ is not an accurate predictor of success.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

IQ offers little to explain the different destinies of people with roughly
 equal promises, schooling, and opportunity
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (Goleman, 1995, p.
 34).
 To elaborate, there are many instances in which people with high IQ struggle,
 while individuals with modest IQ do surprisingly well.
 Instead, Goleman claims that individuals with high degrees of self-awareness,
 self-discipline, and empathy, factors all associated with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

emotional intelligence (EI),
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 will actually fair much better.
 People with well-developed emotional skills are also more likely to be
 content and effective in their lives.
 Social intelligence is both distinct from academic abilitities and a key
 part of what makes people do well in the practicalities of life.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, EI still faces criticism of its own.
 In fact, some researchers refuse to consider EI as a separate form of intellige
nce.
 In their opinion, EI is just another factor they associate with general
 intelligence.
 The few incremental validity studies conducted on EI have shown that adds
 little or nothing to the explanation or prediction of common outcomes,
 such as academic success.
 Also, research of EI and job performance show mixed results.
 In essence, critics assert that the claims of EI's predictive power are
 too extreme.
 Finally, many individuals affirm that the tests created to measure EI actually
 measure traits associated with personality and general intelligence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Despite the misgivings attributed to EI, many individuals believe that EI
 education can provide beneficial skills and tools.
 Childhood is a critical time for the development of EI.
 Yet, some schools across the country have been apprehensive in applying
 new theories and methods to improve the quality of education.
 Schools have become too conerned with academic intelligence, which primarily
 includes reading and math skills, and ranking students based on arbitrary
 standardized tests.
 As a result, teachers, administrators, school boards, etc., have faced harsh
 criticism.
 Many experts believe that school's are doing a poor job of actually fostering
 a child's interests and cultivating his innate natural gifts.
 Schools are failing to educate students in important life skills, and they
 are oblivious to the many different abilities that will help a child succeed.
 Moreover, schools fail to account for the different kinds of intelligences
 that are crucial for life success.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there are still many districts, and even entire states, in the
 United States that are trying to establish social and emotional learning
 (SEL) programs.
 They are even trying to make SEL a curriculum requirement, mandating that
 students should master these essential skills for living.
 For instance, in Illinois, specific learning standards in SEL abilities
 have been established for every grade from kindergarten through the last
 year of high school.
 Preliminary evidence suggests that these programs not only improve behavior,
 but they also improve measurable academic achievement.
 Though, the researchers obtained the data for these studies using massive
 surveys, so the serious problems of endogeneity and measurment error are
 ever present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, is emotional intelligence actually a good predictor of academic success?
 My field experiment would be implemented in order to test the effect EI
 education has on overall academic performance.
 The experiment would mirror the one implemented by Project STAR, which
 was a large-scale randomized experiment designed to measure the effect
 of class size on academic performance.
 Krueger (1999) provides an econometric analysis of the data.
 Thus, for my research design, kindergarten students will be randomly assigned
 to one of two groups: students who participate in SEL programs and students
 who do not participate in SEL programs.
 Students who enter into the experiment in later grades will also be randomly
 assigned.
 After the initial assignment, student's will remain in their group for
 four years.
 At the end of each school year, students will be given a set of standardized
 tests; the results of these tests, along with final grade point averages,
 will be analyzed.
 The ultimate goal of random assignment is to have any and all unobserved
 characteristics be orthogonal to treatment.
 This affords me the opportunity to obtain unbiased estimates on the effect
 SEL programs have on academic success.
\end_layout

